Z||000001||lowCG||03-29-2000||10:36 PM||||Why would you want to go and do all that?<P>Ground control sells a kit with a weld on sleeve and threaded collar to go onto a stock strut if you really feel the need to raise and lower your car.<P>I just put mine together,put the car back on the ground,laughed at it,measured once,made some spacers,and then put'em in; done deal.<P>I spent my $ on some decent shocks instead,but the supertrapps are pretty neat!||||reg||1
Z||000002||DaRkLoRD||03-29-2000||11:59 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by lowCG:<BR><B>Ground control sells a kit with a weld on sleeve and threaded collar to go onto a stock strut if you really feel the need to raise and lower your car.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How much does this cost, and does it affect the handling in the same way that lowering springs do?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||1FST2M6||03-30-2000||12:14 AM|||| <A HREF="http://www.carrerashocks.com" TARGET=_blank>www.carrerashocks.com</A>  they will make you a set just like the ones in the pictures... I've seen many of their threaded body units come through the shop on street and race applications. the Ground control and Skunk Works units will pop out of the perch and bang aginst the shock body and the upper strut mount... Ask any Civic, Miata, Stratus etc...  owner with these kits.. they'll all say the same thing... "Their noisey"  They will drop your car up to 3.5" but only in the rear.. and it's big bucks to change the front to coil overs. Coil overs are nice if you're willing to take the wheel off ever time you want to change your ride... Just get nice a set of replacement springs and Konis.. this will aid in the ride and hnadeling more than the coil overs... unless you just want to SLAM the car.. then coil overs are the ONLY way to go.. Oh carrera will set you up with any spring rate you wish and any shock valving you'd like too... ||||reg||1
